A refusal of one pair applies none of them, and writes nothing: the view of the output finds no file.

```console
$ stetdoc replace contract.docx "thirty days" "sixty days" "ninety days" "sixty days" -o edited.docx --author "Jane Doe"
? 3
error[not_found]: pair 2: "ninety days" is not in the document as it reads now.
next: Nothing was applied. Write OLD exactly as the document reads now (stetdoc view FILE --find WORD shows it), or widen --range or --clause.

$ stetdoc view edited.docx
? 3
error[not_found]: There is no file at edited.docx.
next: Check the path, and that the file is readable.

```

Two pairs that share text are refused, and so is an OLD without its NEW.

```console
$ stetdoc replace contract.docx "within thirty" "within sixty" "thirty days" "sixty days" --dry-run --author "Jane Doe"
? 2
error[invalid_payload]: pairs 1 and 2 overlap in p4, and one text cannot be replaced twice.
next: Give one pair for the text they share.

$ stetdoc replace contract.docx "thirty days" "sixty days" "English law" --dry-run --author "Jane Doe"
? 2
error[invalid_payload]: replace takes OLD NEW pairs, and 3 texts were given, so the last OLD has no NEW.
next: Give a NEW after each OLD. An empty NEW ("") deletes OLD.

```

Places in one paragraph are applied from its end, so every offset holds, and the receipt is JSON with `--json`.

```console
$ stetdoc replace contract.docx "a" "A" --range p1..p1 --all --dry-run --author "Jane Doe"
