A working copy is written by the replace, and refuses -o and --in-place.

```console
$ stetdoc unpack contract.docx
wrote contract.stet: document.md
base 5737b772923cf8d3:0:5737b772923cf8d3
next: stetdoc view contract.stet

$ stetdoc replace contract.stet "English law" "the law of New York" --author "Jane Doe"
replace "English law" with "the law of New York": once, at p5
applied 1 operation, tracked as Jane Doe 2026-10-06T09:30:00Z. base 5737b772923cf8d3:1:dbe1a8673934033e
0 replace p5: deletion #0, insertion #1
p5 This Agreement is governed by {--English law--}{#0 JD}{++the law of New York++}{#1 JD}.
checked 1 unit.

$ stetdoc replace contract.stet "thirty" "sixty" --in-place --author "Jane Doe"
? 2
error[invalid_payload]: --in-place writes a .docx back to itself, and contract.stet is a working copy, which an edit always writes.
next: Drop --in-place, and run stetdoc pack after the edit to write the .docx.

$ stetdoc comment contract.stet "New York" "Confirm with counsel." --author "Jane Doe"
comment on "New York": once, at p5
applied 1 operation, tracked as Jane Doe 2026-10-06T09:30:00Z. base 5737b772923cf8d3:2:5a13a73d3a03e7e2
0 comment p5: c2 added
p5 This Agreement is governed by {--English law--}{#0 JD}{++the law of {c2}New York{/c2}++}{#1 JD}.
checked 1 unit.

```
